1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,848
In Lesson 3, we squeezed the field of our
reference motor into one number:

2
00:00:05,888 --> 00:00:08,534
about 0.94 tesla in the air gap.

3
00:00:08,574 --> 00:00:13,769
But a stator tooth doesn't see one number.
As the rotor turns, it sees a waveform.

4
00:00:13,809 --> 00:00:16,337
Today we'll compute that waveform: its
shape,

5
00:00:16,377 --> 00:00:20,048
the dips the slot openings cut into it,
and its harmonics.

6
00:00:20,088 --> 00:00:23,535
And we'll do it with a field solver you
can run yourself.

7
00:00:24,635 --> 00:00:26,783
First, last lesson's answers.

8
00:00:26,823 --> 00:00:30,248
One: eddy-current loss scales with
lamination thickness squared,

9
00:00:30,288 --> 00:00:33,139
so halving the thickness cuts it to a
quarter.

10
00:00:33,179 --> 00:00:36,496
Two: at the same flux density, doubling
the frequency

11
00:00:36,536 --> 00:00:40,506
doubles the hysteresis loss and quadruples
the eddy loss.

12
00:00:40,546 --> 00:00:50,225
Three: four pole pairs at 5000 rpm give 4
× 5000 / 60 ≈ 333 Hz in the stator iron.

13
00:00:50,265 --> 00:00:54,342
That frequency is set by the rotor's
field: today's subject.

14
00:00:55,442 --> 00:00:59,397
Unroll the air gap: magnets below, the
stator bore above.

15
00:00:59,437 --> 00:01:04,198
Lesson 3's circuit says B is about 0.94
tesla under each magnet:

16
00:01:04,238 --> 00:01:07,598
out of a north magnet, into a south one.

17
00:01:07,638 --> 00:01:10,621
The simplest picture is a flat-topped
wave,

18
00:01:10,661 --> 00:01:16,083
spanning each magnet's 160 electrical
degrees, and zero between magnets.

19
00:01:16,123 --> 00:01:20,863
With a smooth bore, we can do better:
expand the magnets in Fourier harmonics,

20
00:01:20,903 --> 00:01:24,388
and solve the field equations exactly for
each one.

21
00:01:24,428 --> 00:01:31,089
The top stays flat at 0.943 tesla, Lesson
3's value, but the corners round off.

22
00:01:32,189 --> 00:01:37,537
Real stators have slots, and then there's
no tidy formula. So we solve numerically.

23
00:01:37,577 --> 00:01:41,596
Cover one quarter of the motor with a
polar grid:

24
00:01:41,636 --> 00:01:45,558
137 rings by 360 spokes, finest in the
gap.

25
00:01:45,598 --> 00:01:49,170
The unknown at each node is the axial
vector potential, A.

26
00:01:49,210 --> 00:01:53,617
Its contours are field lines, with equal
flux between neighbours.

27
00:01:53,657 --> 00:01:58,397
Around each node, Ampère's law: the line
integral of H around a small cell

28
00:01:58,437 --> 00:02:03,901
equals the current inside. Magnets enter
as equivalent currents at their edges.

29
00:02:03,941 --> 00:02:06,649
That's one linear equation per node:

30
00:02:06,689 --> 00:02:10,866
48,600 sparse equations, solved in about a
second.

31
00:02:10,906 --> 00:02:17,162
The steel is linear, with μr = 4000: an
assumption we'll revisit.

32
00:02:18,262 --> 00:02:21,654
Here's the result, with a north magnet
centred on a tooth.

33
00:02:21,694 --> 00:02:26,202
Flux leaves the north magnet, crosses the
gap, climbs the tooth, splits along

34
00:02:26,242 --> 00:02:31,031
the stator yoke, and returns through the
south magnets and the rotor steel.

35
00:02:31,071 --> 00:02:35,361
The colour shows the flux density. The
teeth carry about 1.5 tesla,

36
00:02:35,401 --> 00:02:39,186
right at the knee of the steel's curve
from Lesson 4.

37
00:02:39,226 --> 00:02:42,349
So linear steel is a little optimistic
here.

38
00:02:43,449 --> 00:02:47,389
Before trusting a solver, test it on
problems with known answers.

39
00:02:47,429 --> 00:02:53,227
With a smooth bore, it matches the
analytic solution within 0.34 percent.

40
00:02:53,267 --> 00:02:57,011
With real steel and air between the
magnets,

41
00:02:57,051 --> 00:03:03,258
its pole-centre field is 0.942 tesla.
Lesson 3's circuit gave 0.941.

42
00:03:03,298 --> 00:03:08,099
Doubling the grid resolution changes the
fundamental by 0.03 percent,

43
00:03:08,139 --> 00:03:11,025
and two other tests agree as well.

44
00:03:12,125 --> 00:03:15,516
Now zoom in where one magnet ends and the
next begins.

45
00:03:15,556 --> 00:03:20,208
The field can't flip instantly. It goes
from plus to minus 90 percent

46
00:03:20,248 --> 00:03:25,571
over about 3.8 millimetres: close to the
magnet's thickness plus the gap.

47
00:03:25,611 --> 00:03:29,929
And some field lines never reach the
stator. They loop straight from one

48
00:03:29,969 --> 00:03:34,687
magnet to its neighbour: leakage, about 4
percent of each magnet's flux.

49
00:03:34,727 --> 00:03:36,924
That's why the flat top has sloped sides,

50
00:03:36,964 --> 00:03:40,682
and Lesson 3's number holds only near the
middle of a pole.

51
00:03:41,782 --> 00:03:46,213
Next, the slot openings: 2-millimetre gaps
between the tooth tips.

52
00:03:46,253 --> 00:03:48,856
Over an opening, the path through air is
longer,

53
00:03:48,896 --> 00:03:52,285
so the flux swerves sideways, toward the
tooth corners.

54
00:03:52,325 --> 00:03:55,798
Under the opening, B in the middle of the
gap drops to two

55
00:03:55,838 --> 00:03:59,893
thirds of its plateau, and at the bore
itself, to 57 percent.

56
00:03:59,933 --> 00:04:03,241
Beside the opening, the corners collect
that flux:

57
00:04:03,281 --> 00:04:06,520
about 1.5 tesla just in front of the
steel.

58
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:10,828
These dips belong to the stator: they stay
put while the rotor turns.

59
00:04:11,928 --> 00:04:16,576
On average, slot openings act like a
slightly wider gap. F. W.

60
00:04:16,616 --> 00:04:21,966
Carter worked out how much in 1900 and
1901, with a conformal map.

61
00:04:22,006 --> 00:04:25,935
The effective gap is the real one times
Carter's coefficient:

62
00:04:25,975 --> 00:04:30,008
the slot pitch, over the slot pitch minus
γ times the gap.

63
00:04:30,048 --> 00:04:33,863
γ depends only on the ratio of slot
opening to gap.

64
00:04:33,903 --> 00:04:39,378
But which gap? With the half-millimetre
air gap, Kc is 1.09.

65
00:04:39,418 --> 00:04:43,807
A surface magnet, though, is almost air.

66
00:04:43,847 --> 00:04:52,068
The rotor steel is g + hm/μrec away: 3.4
millimetres. Then Kc is 1.018.

67
00:04:52,108 --> 00:04:56,210
The solver gives 1.022. So the slot
openings cost this

68
00:04:56,250 --> 00:05:00,816
motor about 2 percent of its average gap
flux, not 8.

69
00:05:01,916 --> 00:05:04,870
Back to the waveform. It repeats every
pole pair,

70
00:05:04,910 --> 00:05:08,448
so write it as a Fourier series in
electrical angle.

71
00:05:08,488 --> 00:05:13,067
For an ideal flat top of height B and
width α,

72
00:05:13,107 --> 00:05:18,187
harmonic n has amplitude 4/(nπ) · B ·
sin(nα/2).

73
00:05:18,227 --> 00:05:22,692
Only odd harmonics appear, because each
south half mirrors the north half.

74
00:05:22,732 --> 00:05:28,027
The computed wave has a fundamental of
1.16 tesla, a little below the ideal,

75
00:05:28,067 --> 00:05:32,568
with weaker 3rd and 5th harmonics: its
corners are rounded.

76
00:05:32,608 --> 00:05:37,053
The slot openings lower the fundamental 2
percent, to 1.14 tesla,

77
00:05:37,093 --> 00:05:42,216
and add small components that come from
the slots, not the magnets.

78
00:05:43,316 --> 00:05:46,128
Now let the rotor turn, and watch one
tooth.

79
00:05:46,168 --> 00:05:49,506
Its flux density swings between plus and
minus

80
00:05:49,546 --> 00:05:52,991
1.5 tesla as north and south magnets pass.

81
00:05:53,031 --> 00:05:58,052
One full cycle takes one pole pair: a
quarter turn, for this 8-pole rotor.

82
00:05:58,092 --> 00:06:02,395
So the electrical frequency is the number
of pole pairs

83
00:06:02,435 --> 00:06:07,533
times the revolutions per second: f =
p·n/60, with n in rpm.

84
00:06:07,573 --> 00:06:09,950
At 3000 rpm, that's 200 hertz:

85
00:06:09,990 --> 00:06:16,131
a 5-millisecond cycle in every tooth and
in every coil's flux linkage.

86
00:06:17,231 --> 00:06:20,907
You can map this field yourself. Take the
rotor out and mount

87
00:06:20,948 --> 00:06:25,024
it on an indexing fixture: a printed
protractor or a stepper.

88
00:06:25,064 --> 00:06:29,069
Lay a transverse Hall probe flat on the
magnets, sensor about half a

89
00:06:29,109 --> 00:06:33,633
millimetre above the surface, and step the
rotor one degree at a time.

90
00:06:33,673 --> 00:06:39,210
Without the stator, expect about 0.33
tesla over each magnet centre,

91
00:06:39,250 --> 00:06:45,351
rising toward 0.37 near the edges, and a
sign change every 45 degrees.

92
00:06:45,391 --> 00:06:48,554
That's about a third of the field in the
motor.

93
00:06:48,594 --> 00:06:54,134
The stator's steel is what lets the
magnets drive 0.94 tesla across the gap.

94
00:06:54,174 --> 00:06:56,429
Then run the solver without the stator,

95
00:06:56,469 --> 00:06:59,676
and compare: a weak magnet stands out at
once.

96
00:07:00,776 --> 00:07:02,464
This solution has limits.

97
00:07:02,504 --> 00:07:05,779
It's two-dimensional: the stack is
infinitely long,

98
00:07:05,819 --> 00:07:10,729
so flux leaking around the ends of the
25-millimetre stack is missing.

99
00:07:10,769 --> 00:07:13,570
The steel is linear, but the teeth sit at
the knee,

100
00:07:13,610 --> 00:07:15,976
so the real field is a little lower.

101
00:07:16,016 --> 00:07:19,545
There's no stator current yet, so no
armature reaction.

102
00:07:19,585 --> 00:07:23,719
And the magnets are ideal: uniform, at
room temperature.

103
00:07:24,819 --> 00:07:27,099
Three predictions before next time.

104
00:07:27,139 --> 00:07:31,477
One: make the slot openings wider. What
happens to Carter's coefficient,

105
00:07:31,517 --> 00:07:34,631
and to the average flux density in the
gap?

106
00:07:35,731 --> 00:07:40,230
Two: a flat-topped wave of height B spans
160 electrical degrees.

107
00:07:40,270 --> 00:07:43,563
What's the amplitude of its fundamental?

108
00:07:44,663 --> 00:07:51,052
Three: at 1500 rpm, what's the electrical
frequency of this field past a tooth?

109
00:07:52,152 --> 00:07:57,013
Next time: how the winding filters these
harmonics, with winding factors.
